{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-215707",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $\\frac{5-9a}{2}$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $7.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-9a}{2} & \\le -7 & \\text{or} &  & \\frac{5-9a}{2} & \\ge 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-9a}{2} & \\le -7 &  &  & \\frac{5-9a}{2} & \\ge 7 \\\\ 5-9a & \\le -14 &  &  & 5-9a & \\ge 14 \\\\ -9a & \\le -19 &  &  & -9a & \\ge 9 \\\\ 9a & \\ge 19 &  &  & 9a & \\le -9 \\\\ a & \\ge \\frac{19}{9} &  &  & a & \\le -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & \\le -1 & \\text{or} &  & a & \\ge \\frac{19}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
